Tests for the patches in patches/gnome-text-editor/ and patches/gtk4/, and for
patches/mutter/save-toplevels-on-session-destroy.patch with them, run by hand.

Text Editor session restore: run-text-editor-session.sh, text_editor_session.py and fake_session_manager.py
    Checks that Text Editor, with GTK's save/restore API, names its windows to mutter and has them restored under
    the same names at the next start. Starts a headless mutter with one virtual monitor in a D-Bus session of its
    own and runs Text Editor three times in it, with GSettings in memory, so that its defaults apply. The first run
    opens two files, each in a window of its own, and quits through the app.quit action. The second run starts
    without files, which restores the session. The third run starts with --ignore-session, which opens one new
    window instead, so Text Editor has to destroy the two windows it created for GTK's restored window state without
    showing them, and must keep running. It covers the protocol between GTK and mutter and the link between GTK's
    window state and Text Editor's session state, but not whether windows return to their workspaces, which needs a
    desktop. The test also runs next to your own Text Editor without touching it.

    Mutter's binary is only in the mutter package, and Text Editor and GTK are tested without installing them, so
    all three are extracted, mutter from the local repository and the other two from the build to test:

      mkdir /tmp/mutter-root /tmp/text-editor-root
      for p in mutter libmutter-18-0; do dpkg-deb -x /usr/local/lib/debs/${p}_*.deb /tmp/mutter-root; done
      for p in gnome-text-editor libgtk-4-1; do dpkg-deb -x /usr/local/lib/debs/${p}_*.deb /tmp/text-editor-root; done
      ./run-text-editor-session.sh /tmp/mutter-root /tmp/text-editor-root /tmp/text-editor-session-test

    The fourth argument says which session manager the D-Bus session has. With unsupported, the default,
    fake_session_manager.py stands in for gnome-session and reports RestoreSupported as false, as gnome-session 50
    does, and GTK must not register with it. With supported it reports true, and GTK must register at every start
    and keep its state under the instance ID the session manager returns. With none there is no session manager.

    In each run GTK sends get_session, the first time for a new session and then with the session ID mutter
    created the first time, and names each window with restore_toplevel, which in the second run uses the names of
    the first and is answered with restored(). The third run names only its new window, under a new name. The
    script prints what each run sent and received, the window IDs and names GTK saved, the windows Text Editor
    saved, one line for each check, and finally "RESULT: OK", and exits with 0. With Ubuntu's GTK, Text Editor
    sends nothing over the protocol and the checks fail from the first one. Without
    patches/mutter/save-toplevels-on-session-destroy.patch, only the first window is restored in the second run,
    since mutter forgets where the other one was when Text Editor quits with it open.

    The full WAYLAND_DEBUG output of each run is in the output directory as run1.log, run2.log and run3.log, and the
    calls the session manager received are in session-manager.log. The D-Bus and portal messages printed on the way
    are expected in the private session.

Text Editor auto-save: run-text-editor-autosave.sh and text_editor_autosave.py
    Checks that Text Editor autosaves its session, with the drafts of unsaved documents, a few seconds after every
    change, as set by auto-save-delay, also when that setting is not its default. Starts a headless mutter with one
    virtual monitor in a D-Bus session of its own, and Text Editor in it once for each scenario, each time with XDG
    homes of its own and GSettings read from a keyfile there, with the schema of the Text Editor tested. Each
    scenario waits for the session saved after the start, opens two files one after the other in the running
    instance, waits each time for the session saved with that file, and quits through the app.quit action, which
    saves the session as well.

    default-delay leaves auto-save-delay at its default of 3 seconds. delay-set-before-start sets it to 5 seconds
    before Text Editor starts. Text Editor binds the setting while it starts, before it enables auto-save, so the
    session changes while auto-save is still disabled. When _editor_session_mark_dirty then marks the session dirty
    without scheduling an auto-save, enabling auto-save and every later change find the session dirty already, and
    only quitting saves it, so every autosave check of that scenario fails. Text Editor 50.1 does that.

    delay-changed-while-pending changes auto-save-delay from 3 to 4 seconds with gsettings one second after the first
    file opened, while its auto-save is pending. Changing the delay removes the pending auto-save, and when
    _editor_session_mark_dirty then finds the session dirty already, it schedules none with the new delay, so neither
    file is autosaved. A save sooner than the new delay after the change is the one scheduled before, which means
    Text Editor did not notice the change, and fails the check that the save followed the new delay.

    slow-draft-save sets auto-save-delay to 1 second and passes 24 MB of text to Text Editor on standard input, with
    gnome-text-editor -, which opens it as an unsaved draft. Text Editor writes a draft at a few megabytes a second,
    so the save which writes it lasts several seconds. A save captures the session state when it is requested and
    writes it after its drafts, and writing a draft for the first time adds it to the session's list of drafts and
    requests another save, so only the state of that later save lists the draft. When saves run at the same time, the
    later one has no draft to write and writes its state first, and the first one then writes its older state over
    it, so the session saved last does not list the draft. The scenario checks that the draft was still being
    written when the later save was requested, and fails when the machine writes the draft faster than that, since
    then it tests nothing. It ends Text Editor with SIGTERM, since quitting asks what to do with the unsaved draft.

    ROOT is a directory Text Editor was installed into, for example with meson install --destdir, or its package was
    extracted into, with libgtk-4-1 extracted there too when it is to run with that GTK:

      ./run-text-editor-autosave.sh /tmp/mutter-root /tmp/text-editor-root /tmp/text-editor-autosave-test

    The script prints one line for each check, with the seconds from the change to the save, and finally
    "RESULT: OK", and exits with 0. Text Editor's output of each scenario is in SCENARIO/text-editor.log in the
    output directory.
